WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.659 --> 00:00:06.259
When YouTube mogul PewDiePie visited The Late
Show with Stephen Colbert in October 2015,

00:00:06.259 --> 00:00:08.120
the host said:

00:00:08.120 --> 00:00:13.750
“Um, I want to thank the internet for allowing
their emperor to be here for the evening.”

00:00:13.750 --> 00:00:19.160
That obviously was a joke, but if we had to
elect an internet leader, PewDiePie — a.k.a.

00:00:19.160 --> 00:00:22.490
Felix Kjellberg — would certainly be in
the running.

00:00:22.490 --> 00:00:27.309
The 26-year-old native of Sweden claims almost
50 million subscribers on his YouTube channel

00:00:27.309 --> 00:00:30.270
— the most of anyone by miles.

00:00:30.270 --> 00:00:35.261
While even your grandparents might have confusedly
heard the name PewDiePie at least once, how

00:00:35.261 --> 00:00:37.690
many people really know his story?

00:00:37.690 --> 00:00:41.000
Here are the gritty details behind YouTube's
happiest superstar.

00:00:41.000 --> 00:00:42.309
A calling card

00:00:42.309 --> 00:00:48.079
PewDiePie, who found fame when he started
recording himself playing video games, gave

00:00:48.079 --> 00:00:50.060
himself his oft-squealed nickname …

00:00:50.060 --> 00:00:51.060
“PewDiePie”

00:00:51.060 --> 00:00:54.719
... because of its resemblance to a common
sound effect found in those games.

00:00:54.719 --> 00:00:56.289
“It’s like any gamer, I guess.

00:00:56.289 --> 00:00:57.670
We all have our nicknames.

00:00:57.670 --> 00:01:02.720
I would nick Pewdie, cuz ‘pew’ like the
lasers...pew pew!

00:01:02.720 --> 00:01:07.970
Um, and I would yell out “PewDiePie” whenever
I did well in games.

00:01:07.970 --> 00:01:10.660
And people would just laugh, and I just went
with it.”

00:01:10.660 --> 00:01:15.640
Kjellberg's first YouTube channel, launched
in 2006, was simply called PewDie (without

00:01:15.640 --> 00:01:16.840
the pie).

00:01:16.840 --> 00:01:20.930
That second syllable means exactly what you
think it means: "Die."

00:01:20.930 --> 00:01:24.310
As in, characters in the games die when the
lasers come out.

00:01:24.310 --> 00:01:29.600
However, he re-launched in 2010 after losing
his password to the first account.

00:01:29.600 --> 00:01:35.680
He added the "Pie" part because, well, he
likes pie … among other edible items.

00:01:35.680 --> 00:01:37.020
Lots-o-loot

00:01:37.020 --> 00:01:43.900
$7.4 million a year seems like a lot of money
for a college dropout who worked at a hotdog

00:01:43.900 --> 00:01:46.520
stand to make cash while playing video games.

00:01:46.520 --> 00:01:51.230
In fact, $7.4 million is a lot of money for
anyone to make.

00:01:51.230 --> 00:01:55.470
But according to some analysts, it's nowhere
near the salary Kjellberg should be pulling

00:01:55.470 --> 00:01:56.470
right now.

00:01:56.470 --> 00:02:00.470
Given his massive subscriber base and the
amount of ad revenue that YouTube is pulling

00:02:00.470 --> 00:02:04.920
in from its video plays, experts say he's
actually way underpaid.

00:02:04.920 --> 00:02:09.090
Even so, Kjellberg rarely speaks about his
financial earnings from the site because he

00:02:09.090 --> 00:02:12.030
says that he didn't get into this business
for the money.

00:02:12.030 --> 00:02:17.560
“I didn’t know you could be big at...well
I know people who are big at other type of

00:02:17.560 --> 00:02:21.190
videos, but there was no one big at gaming,
and I didn’t know you could make money out

00:02:21.190 --> 00:02:22.190
of it.

00:02:22.190 --> 00:02:26.250
So it was never, like, a career that I just
quit college to pursue.”

00:02:26.250 --> 00:02:30.940
However, he was still none too pleased when
YouTube's monetization policy changed, and

00:02:30.940 --> 00:02:32.870
he spoke out against the company's decision.

00:02:32.870 --> 00:02:38.140
“If you don’t know what happened, basically
YouTube started unmonetizing people’s videos

00:02:38.140 --> 00:02:39.760
without any notice.”

00:02:39.760 --> 00:02:41.780
Parental approval

00:02:41.780 --> 00:02:46.511
Before he made it big online, Kjellberg's
parents weren't so keen on the idea of him

00:02:46.511 --> 00:02:49.700
making a living by playing video games.

00:02:49.700 --> 00:02:53.950
They were both highly esteemed executives
in Sweden at the time and seemed to hope he'd

00:02:53.950 --> 00:02:59.319
follow in their own footsteps by sending him
to Chalmers University of Technology for industrial

00:02:59.319 --> 00:03:00.510
economics.

00:03:00.510 --> 00:03:03.350
But the corporate lifestyle simply wasn't
for him.

00:03:03.350 --> 00:03:08.590
“Shortly after, I quit college, and so I
had to get a job, and I took whatever job

00:03:08.590 --> 00:03:12.210
I could get, which was to work in a hotdog
stand.”

00:03:12.210 --> 00:03:17.010
But now that he's proven the worth of this
business, his family seems to have come around.

00:03:17.010 --> 00:03:21.790
His mother and sister have both made appearances
on his YouTube channel, although his dad still

00:03:21.790 --> 00:03:24.230
seems a bit camera shy.

00:03:24.230 --> 00:03:25.740
The Bro Army

00:03:25.740 --> 00:03:31.590
The term "bro" has all kinds of negative meathead
connotations here in the United States, but

00:03:31.590 --> 00:03:32.750
Kjellberg uses it a lot.

00:03:32.750 --> 00:03:34.430
“Hey how’s it going bros?”

00:03:34.430 --> 00:03:35.430
“Hey how’s it going bros?”

00:03:35.430 --> 00:03:36.430
“Hey how’s it going bros?”

00:03:36.430 --> 00:03:39.380
Some of his followers have begun to live up
to the connotation.

00:03:39.380 --> 00:03:44.069
When other YouTubers play a game that PewDiePie
has already covered, the Bro Army sometimes

00:03:44.069 --> 00:03:48.080
comes after them in force for "copying" their
virtual leader.

00:03:48.080 --> 00:03:53.310
PewDiePie himself responded by posting a video
mocking his own fans and disabling comments

00:03:53.310 --> 00:03:56.650
on his own uploads for awhile.

00:03:56.650 --> 00:03:58.610
PewDiePhilanthropist

00:03:58.610 --> 00:04:04.150
As harsh as the Bros can be and as much flack
as PewDiePie has gotten for making millions,

00:04:04.150 --> 00:04:06.690
he and his fans have done a lot of good.

00:04:06.690 --> 00:04:13.120
Kjellberg and the Bro Army have already raised
over $630,000 for Save the Children, over

00:04:13.120 --> 00:04:19.200
$446,000 for Charity: Water, and tens of thousands
for other organizations.

00:04:19.200 --> 00:04:25.490
“Putting all those together, we have reached
over $1 million towards charities.

00:04:25.490 --> 00:04:29.450
How frickin’...that is...ah, I can’t...I
can’t…”

00:04:29.450 --> 00:04:32.160
PewDiePie's Cutie Pie

00:04:32.160 --> 00:04:37.710
Kjellberg's romance with girlfriend Marzia
is a fairy tale for the digital age.

00:04:37.710 --> 00:04:42.610
In 2011, the young Italian woman became familiar
with PewDie's work through some friends, and

00:04:42.610 --> 00:04:46.100
became taken with him and wrote him a fan
e-mail.

00:04:46.100 --> 00:04:50.720
It didn't take long for him to respond and
then fly out to Italy for a real-life date.

00:04:50.720 --> 00:04:56.380
Within a year, Marzia moved to Sweden and
started a YouTube channel of her own, called

00:04:56.380 --> 00:04:57.380
CutiePieMarzia.

00:04:57.380 --> 00:05:02.480
The two bounced back to Italy for awhile before
settling in England with their two pugs, Edgar

00:05:02.480 --> 00:05:03.480
and Maya.

00:05:03.480 --> 00:05:07.210
Not a bad journey for a video game-playing
hot dog salesman!

00:05:07.210 --> 00:05:08.270
Thanks for watching!

